        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Match the belt to your machine, not to a listing.</strong> Find the
            model and serial number, look up the belt's part number if you can, and measure the old
            belt's loop length, width and seam. Then buy from a listing that agrees with all of it,
            such as SportSmith's serial-matched belts, an ICON original part or a size-stated belt
            like the Sosation.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check the deck and rollers before you fit it, lubricate only as the maker directs, and
            expect to re-tension and re-centre after the first few sessions. For the job itself, see
            our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">replacing a treadmill belt</a>.
          </p>
